# Target C-II — phase-continuity upgrades mixed flower matching from one third to one half

**Date:** 2026-09-15\
**Status:** proof-only asymptotic sharpening of the mixed matching-splice theorem\
**Scope:** local through pairs supplied by the three-dominant full-flower normal form\
**Dependencies:** `target_c_mixed_flower_matching_splice_deficit_2026-09-15.md`; oriented through continuation at every selected flower\
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The preceding mixed-phase theorem used only the maximum-degree-two property of the
splice graph and therefore kept a greedy `L/3` matching.  The geometric through-pair
input excludes all short splice cycles.  Since the phase mismatch on every local
through edge tends uniformly to zero, the number of cycle components is `o(D)`.
Therefore almost half of all local through pairs can be selected vertex-disjointly.

If `L_D` local through-pair flowers satisfy the hypotheses of the mixed-splice theorem,
then

\[
\boxed{
P_D\ge {L_D\over2}-o(D)
}
\tag{HM.1}
\]

matched splices can be selected.  The already-audited two-piece supertrain theorem
then gives

\[
\boxed{
{T_D^2\over4}-R_D
\ge {L_D^2\over8}-o(D^2).
}
\tag{HM.2}

For `M_D` full flowers with `C_D` complement terminal occurrences relative to the
dominant orbit, the three-dominant local normal form supplies

\[
L_D\ge M_D-C_D/3-o(D),
\]

so

\[
\boxed{
{T_D^2\over4}-R_D
\ge {1\over8}
\left(M_D-{C_D\over3}\right)_+^2-o(D^2).
}
\tag{HM.3}

This is a factor `9/4` stronger than the prior `1/18` coefficient and is the natural
matching constant for a degree-two splice system.

---

## 1. Oriented through edges preserve the phase lift

For every selected local through pair, choose oriented contact representatives on its
two stopped trains so that their positive train normals differ by at most

\[
\epsilon_D\to0
\]

uniformly over all selected flowers.  The connector joins an endpoint at which the
first positive-normal train enters the face to an endpoint from which the second
positive-normal train leaves.  Thus it gives a directed continuation

\[
\tau\longrightarrow\sigma.
\]

Flipping both oriented representatives reverses the same directed continuation; it
does not turn a through connection into a U-turn.  In particular the relation between
the two oriented projective lifts is preserving, not antipodal.

A complete train has one tail terminal and one head terminal in its positive-normal
orientation.  Hence in the directed splice graph every train vertex has

\[
\deg^+\le1,
\qquad
\deg^-\le1.
\tag{HM.4}
\]

An underlying degree-two cycle is therefore a directed cycle after choosing one
orientation around it.

---

## 2. A short directed splice cycle has impossible positive projection

Consider a directed splice cycle with train vertices

\[
\tau_1,\ldots,\tau_\ell.
\]

Choose the oriented contact direction `u_1` on `tau_1`.  Propagate the oriented lift
around the cycle using the through continuations.  Consecutive directions differ by at
most `epsilon_D`, so before the cycle closes

\[
\angle(u_j,u_1)\le (j-1)\epsilon_D.
\tag{HM.5}
\]

Suppose

\[
\ell\epsilon_D<\pi/12.
\tag{HM.6}
\]

Then every train normal on the cycle lies within `pi/12` of

\[
n_1=Ju_1.
\]

Every elementary midpoint displacement on one stopped train lies within strictly
`pi/6` of its own positive normal.  Therefore every train segment used in the directed
cycle lies within

\[
\pi/6+\pi/12=\pi/4
\]

of `n_1` and has strictly positive `n_1` projection.

The selected connector on each splice edge lies within

\[
\pi/6+\epsilon_D
\]

of the corresponding common positive normal, hence within `pi/4+o(1)` of `n_1` under
(HM.6), and also has positive `n_1` projection for large `D`.

Following the directed cycle therefore produces a closed polygonal curve every segment
of which has strictly positive `n_1` projection.  Its total displacement would have
positive `n_1` projection, contradicting closure.

Hence every splice-graph cycle obeys

\[
\boxed{
\ell\ge {\pi\over12\epsilon_D}.
}
\tag{HM.7}

The argument is exactly why the local connectors had to be genuine through
continuations rather than arbitrary chords of the pentagon.

---

## 3. There are only `o(D)` cycle components

The stopped-train count is `O(D)` in the full-core Type-II branch; the exact finite
bound `T_D<=3q=O(D)` is more than enough.  Distinct splice-graph cycle components are
vertex-disjoint.  By (HM.7), if `c_D` is their number,

\[
c_D {\pi\over12\epsilon_D}\le T_D=O(D).
\]

Thus

\[
\boxed{c_D=O(\epsilon_DD)=o(D).}
\tag{HM.8}
\]

No assertion that the splice graph is globally acyclic is made.  Long phase-winding
cycles are permitted; there are simply too few of them to affect the leading matching
density.

---

## 4. Maximum matching in paths and long cycles

A loopless graph of maximum degree two is a disjoint union of paths, cycles, and
parallel-edge two-vertex components.  A path with `e` edges has a matching of size at
least `e/2`.  An even cycle has matching size exactly `e/2`; an odd cycle loses only
`1/2` from that real-valued bound.  A parallel two-edge component has matching size
one, again exactly half its edge count.

Summing componentwise,

\[
P_D\ge {L_D\over2}-{c_D\over2}.
\]

Use (HM.8):

\[
P_D\ge {L_D\over2}-o(D),
\]

which is (HM.1).

---

## 5. Improved global train deficit

The hostile-audited mixed-splice theorem applies to any matching of the local through
pairs.  It gives

\[
{T_D^2\over4}-R_D
\ge {P_D^2\over2}-o(D^2).
\]

Insert (HM.1):

\[
{P_D^2\over2}
\ge {L_D^2\over8}-o(D^2),
\]

proving (HM.2).  Equation (HM.3) follows from the three-dominant allocation
`L_D>=M_D-C_D/3-o(D)`.

A coarse live-row consequence is now

```text
if L_D >= M_D/2:
    global train deficit >= M_D^2/32-o(D^2);
else:
    >3M_D/4-o(D) distinct complement trains terminate on the flowers.
```

At `M_D>0.1327D-o(D)`, the first alternative gives a deficit above

\[
0.1327^2/32>0.0005502
\]

in normalized `D^2` units.

The aggregate flower branch already has a larger unlocalized scalar deficit.  The
value of (HM.2)--(HM.3) is that the deficit is generated by a linear family of actual
through mechanisms, with an asymptotically optimal matching density, and remains valid
without a common splice phase.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only half-matching upgrade conditional on the stated
oriented through-pair hypotheses.  C-II and Level I remain open.
